              {
                "text": "We need to understand rule and verifier, then create 3 demonstration pairs.\n\nVerifier logic:\n- partition(I): groups by color value (each color yields object of all cells with that value). Note includes background 0 as an object too.\n- x1 = fork(multiply, height, width): for each object compute height*width.\n- x2 = argmax(x0, x1): the object with max height*width (largest bounding-box area). This selects the largest square? Actually object is set of cells of a single color. The large square (color 2) has height*width = its bounding area. Background 0 might be huge? Wait background 0 spans the whole grid except the square region, but as a single color object, its bounding box is the entire grid, so height*width = full grid area, which is larger than the square! Hmm, but example output is 6x6 square cropped.\n\nLet's check: input grid 12x10. Background 0 object includes all zeros. Its bounding box: rows 0-11, cols 0-9 => 12*10=120. Color 2 object: rows 4-9, cols 1-6 => 6*6=36. Color 1: 2x2=4. So argmax would choose background 0, not the square. But output is cropped square swapped colors. So maybe partition groups by connected components? Look at partition definition: \"each cell with the same value part of the same object\" returns frozenset of frozenset((v,(i,j)) for ... if v==value). So yes by value. Then background 0 is largest.\n\nBut wait x0 = partition(I) includes all colors. argmax picks max area object. Then x3 = remove(x2, x0), x4 = merge(x3) => merges all other objects. Then x5 = subgrid(x4, I): smallest subgrid containing object x4. x4 is the union of all objects except the largest area object. Hmm.\n\nIf x2 is background 0, then x3 = all non-zero cells (color 2 and 1), merged. x4 = all non-background cells. subgrid(x4, I) crops bounding box of non-zero cells, which is rows 4-9, cols 1-6 => the 6x6 large square containing colors 2 and 1. Then x6 = mostcolor(x5) = 2 (most common in cropped region, since 2 dominates), x7 = leastcolor(x5) = 1. switch swaps 2 and 1. Output is 6x6 with 1 border and 2 center. That matches!\n\nSo the rule: find the object with largest area (background typically), remove it; the remainder forms the shape; crop to bounding box; swap the two most/least common colors. Actually the remainder is the \"foreground\" containing the large square and inner square. Since background is largest area object (whole grid bounding box), that's removed. Then crop to the bounding box of the remaining colors.\n\nWait, in general the \"largest\" object might be background if background is one connected region (by value). The rule in words says: a square with smaller square centered inside, on black background. Output grid same size as larger square. Swap colors of two squares.\n\nSo the transformation: crop out the foreground region (the large square plus inner square), then swap the two colors.\n\nNow I need to create 3 demonstration pairs. They can vary in size, colors, positions. Important: the verifier must evaluate. The input should have a black (0) background, a large square, and a smaller square inside it, two different colors (nonzero). The output is the cropped large square with colors swapped.\n\nLet me design simple ones.\n\nBut careful: The verifier uses partition by color, finds max area object. For that to be background, the background object's bounding box must have area larger than the large square's area. Since background is entire grid, its bounding box is the whole grid, area = H*W. The large square area = s*s. As long as H*W > s*s, background wins. Usually true since background surrounds. But if background doesn't touch edges? Still bounding box of zeros might be large. Actually if large square is placed such that zeros form a ring around it, zeros' bounding box could be the whole grid. So fine.\n\nBut what if background color is not the most common? It's largest by bounding box area. Actually background's bounding box = whole grid if zeros exist in all four corners? Not necessarily. If the square spans the entire grid, no background. But rule says on black background, so square smaller than grid. Zeros exist on all sides typically -> bounding box is full grid. So background area is full grid.\n\nThe remainder (non-background colors) = large square + inner square. Their combined bounding box is the large square. crop yields the large square region. Then mostcolor and leastcolor of that cropped region: the large square color is most common, inner square color is least common (assuming inner smaller). switch swaps them.\n\nSo any valid input: black background, two nested squares.\n\nEdge cases: What if there are more than 2 non-background colors? Rule says two squares different colors. Let's stick to exactly 2 colors.\n\nLet me create 3 pairs with different sizes, colors, positions.\n\nPair 1: 8x8 grid? Let's make input 10x10, large square 4x4 at rows 3-6, cols 3-6, inner 2x2 at rows 4-5, cols 4-5. Colors: large = 3, inner = 4.\n\nInput grid 10x10 all 0 except:\nrows 3-6, cols 3-6 = 3\nrows 4-5, cols 4-5 = 4\n\nOutput: crop rows 3-6, cols 3-6 => 4x4, swap 3 and 4 => border 4, center 3.\n\nLet me verify verifier: background 0 object bounding box rows 0-9, cols 0-9 => 100. Large square 3: 16. Inner 4: 4. argmax picks 0. x4 = union of 3 and 4 objects. subgrid crop to bounding box rows 3-6, cols 3-6. mostcolor=3 (12 cells vs 4), leastcolor=4. switch swaps 3<->4. Output 4x4 with 4 border and 3 center. Good.\n\nPair 2: different shape. Maybe non-square inner? Rule says smaller square, so keep square.\n\nInput 12x12, large square 6x6 at rows 2-7, cols 4-9, color 5. Inner 2x2 at rows 4-5, cols 6-7, color 8. Wait must be centered? Rule says centered inside larger square. But verifier doesn't check centered; it just crops. However for teaching, better to keep centered to convey \"centered inside\". Actually the rule says smaller square centered. So make inner centered.\n\nLarge 6x6 at rows 2-7, cols 4-9. Center is rows 4-4.5? Indices 2,3,4,5,6,7; center rows 4,5 (since even). cols 4-9, center cols 6,7. Inner 2x2 at rows 4-5, cols 6-7. That's centered. Colors: large=5, inner=8.\n\nOutput crop 6x6, swap 5 and 8 => border 8, center 5.\n\nPair 3: Maybe large square 8x8, inner 4x4? Different ratio. Input 14x14, large square 8x8 at rows 3-10, cols 2-9? Wait 8 wide cols 3-10 maybe. Let's make large 6x6 again? Better variety.\n\nLet's do large 5x5 (odd), inner 1x1? Rule says smaller square, can be 1x1. But \"smaller square centered inside\" - 1x1 centered works. Let's do large 5x5 color 6, inner 1x1 color 9 at center.\n\nInput grid 11x11 all 0. Large square 5x5 at rows 3-7, cols 3-7, color 6. Center row 5, col 5 => color 9.\n\nCrop rows 3-7, cols 3-7 => 5x5. mostcolor=6 (24 cells), leastcolor=9 (1 cell). swap => border 9, center 6.\n\nWait, inner 1x1: the output would be 5x5 with all 9 except center 6. That's fine.\n\nBut let's check: does the verifier's leastcolor handle ties? Only 2 colors in cropped region, so fine.\n\nAlternatively, large 4x4 inner 2x2 already done.\n\nLet me also consider background might not be 0? Rule says black background (0). Keep 0.\n\nNow, I need to call show_transformed_input with each input. The environment computes output. I'll write the grids as arrays of arrays.\n\nLet me carefully create the first input.\n\nPair 1: 10x10\nRows 0-2: all 0 (10 zeros)\nRow 3: 0,0,0,3,3,3,3,0,0,0 -> wait cols 3-6 inclusive = 4 cells. indices 3,4,5,6.\nSo row 3: [0,0,0,3,3,3,3,0,0,0]\nRows 4-5: large 3 at cols 3-6, inner 4 at cols 4-5.\nRow 4: [0,0,0,3,4,4,3,0,0,0]\nRow 5: [0,0,0,3,4,4,3,0,0,0]\nRow 6: [0,0,0,3,3,3,3,0,0,0]\nRows 7-9: all 0.\n\nCheck rows 3-6 is 4 rows. So large square is 4x4 at rows 3-6, cols 3-6.\n\nPair 2: 12x12\nLarge 6x6 at rows 2-7, cols 4-9, color 5. Inner 2x2 at rows 4-5, cols 6-7, color 8.\n\nRows 0-1: all 0 (12 zeros)\nRow 2: cols 4-9 = 5. So [0,0,0,0,5,5,5,5,5,5,0,0]\nRow 3: same [0,0,0,0,5,5,5,5,5,5,0,0]\nRow 4: cols 4-5 =5, cols 6-7=8, cols 8-9=5 -> [0,0,0,0,5,5,8,8,5,5,0,0]\nRow 5: same [0,0,0,0,5,5,8,8,5,5,0,0]\nRow 6: [0,0,0,0,5,5,5,5,5,5,0,0]\nRow 7: [0,0,0,0,5,5,5,5,5,5,0,0]\nRows 8-11: all 0.\n\nCheck large 6x6: rows 2,3,4,5,6,7 (6 rows), cols 4,5,6,7,8,9 (6 cols). Yes.\n\nPair 3: 11x11\nLarge 5x5 at rows 3-7, cols 3-7, color 6. Inner 1x1 at row 5, col 5, color 9.\n\nRows 0-2: all 0 (11 zeros)\nRow 3: cols 3-7 = 6 -> [0,0,0,6,6,6,6,6,0,0,0]\nRow 4: same\nRow 5: cols 3-4=6, col5=9, cols6-7=6 -> [0,0,0,6,6,9,6,6,0,0,0]\nRow 6: [0,0,0,6,6,6,6,6,0,0,0]\nRow 7: [0,0,0,6,6,6,6,6,0,0,0]\nRows 8-10: all 0.\n\nWait large 5x5 rows 3-7 inclusive = 5 rows (3,4,5,6,7). cols 3-7 inclusive = 5 cols. Yes.\n\nNow let's submit these.\n\nBut before finalizing, think if there's any trick. The verifier: x2 = argmax(x0, x1). x0 = partition(I) returns frozenset of objects. x1 = fork(multiply, height, width). Wait, fork(outer, a, b) returns lambda x: outer(a(x), b(x)). So x1 = multiply(height(obj), width(obj)). For each object. argmax picks max. If there's a tie? Background is usually largest. Could there be a case where large square area equals background bounding box area? If square touches all edges, background could be less or zero. But we ensure background surrounds.\n\nActually, let's double-check the verification. partition(I) for grid with color 0 and color 3 and color 4. Objects: {0 cells}, {3 cells}, {4 cells}. Height and width of object 0: bounding box of all 0 cells. Since 0 cells are everywhere except the square, bounding box includes all rows and cols? In pair 1, rows 0-9 all have some 0? Row 3 has 0s at cols 0-2 and 7-9, so yes row 3 has zeros. Rows 4-5 have zeros. All rows have zeros. All columns have zeros? col 0-2 zeros, col 3 has 0 in rows 0-2? Actually col 3: rows 0-2 are 0, rows 3-6 are 3, rows 7-9 are 0. So yes col 3 has zeros. Every row and col has zeros. So bounding box is full 10x10 = 100. Good.\n\nIn pair 3, 11x11, large square rows 3-7 cols 3-7. All rows have zeros? Row 3-7 have zeros outside cols 3-7. Rows 0-2 all zeros. Rows 8-10 all zeros. All columns have zeros similarly. So bounding box full 11x11 = 121. Large square 25. Good.\n\nSo outputs should be correct.\n\nLet me also consider if the student could infer the rule from 3 examples. We have different sizes, colors, inner sizes. Should be enough.\n\nNow call show_transformed_input for each. I can make multiple calls in one block. Let's do that.",
